## Passing Structs Across the FFI Boundary

To pass structs to C functions, they must use C-compatible memory layout.

### C-Compatible Structs

```xiom
#[repr(C)]
pub type CPoint = {
  x: Float64;
  y: Float64;
}

extern "C" {
  fn distance(p1: CPoint, p2: CPoint) -> Float64;
}
```

`#[repr(C)]` ensures the struct has the same layout as C would use.

### Passing Struct Pointers

```xiom
#[repr(C)]
pub type CRect = {
  x: Float64;
  y: Float64;
  w: Float64;
  h: Float64;
}

extern "C" {
  fn rect_area(rect: *const CRect) -> Float64;
  fn rect_move(rect: *mut CRect, dx: Float64, dy: Float64) -> Unit;
}

fn compute_area(rect: &CRect) -> Float64 {
  unsafe { return rect_area(rect as *const CRect); }
}
```

### Nested C Structs

```xiom
#[repr(C)]
pub type CAddress = { street: *const Char; city: *const Char; }

#[repr(C)]
pub type CPerson = {
  name: *const Char;
  age: Int;
  addr: CAddress;
}

extern "C" {
  fn print_person(p: *const CPerson) -> Unit;
}
```

### FFI Struct Safety

- Always use `#[repr(C)]` for FFI structs
- Don't use XIOM-specific types (Option, Vec, Str) in C structs
- Use raw pointers for strings: `*const Char` instead of `Str`
- Manual memory management for C allocations

### Boxing FFI Structs

```xiom
#[repr(C)]
pub type COpaque = { _private: [0]Int; }

extern "C" {
  fn create_handle() -> *mut COpaque;
  fn destroy_handle(handle: *mut COpaque) -> Unit;
}
```

